---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import maw2009 from '../assets/images/digital/maw-2009.jpg';
import maw2026 from '../assets/images/digital/maw-2026.jpg';
---

<Layout
	title="Digital Work: The Martin Amis Web"
	description="Gavin Keulks — web design and digital humanities, including the Martin Amis Web."
>
	<div class="wrap">
		<header class="page-head">
			<h1>Digital Work</h1>
			<p class="page-intro">
				Web design and digital humanities.
			</p>
		</header>

		<section class="archive">
			<h2>The Martin Amis Web</h2>
			<p>
				I have overseen and administered{' '}
				<a href="https://martinamisweb.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the Martin&nbsp;Amis Web</a> since
				2006 &mdash; the authoritative digital archive for the author. The British
				Library designated it a site of scholarly significance in 2012 and
				archives it every six months as part of their ongoing UK Web Archive
				project. I rebuilt the site in 2006 and completely overhauled and
				modernized it in 2026.
			</p>

			<figure class="site-shot">
				<a href="https://martinamisweb.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
					<Image
						src={maw2026}
						alt="The Martin Amis Web home page in 2026: a dark header with the site's title and navigation, above an introduction and a black-and-white portrait of Martin Amis"
						widths={[480, 768, 1152, 1536]}
						sizes="(max-width: 53rem) 90vw, 48rem"
					/>
				</a>
				<figcaption>The 2026 overhaul</figcaption>
			</figure>

			<p>
				Administering a digital archive for a famous figure has been not only a
				responsibility but also a subject of my research, spanning numerous
				conference papers and presentations.
			</p>
			<ul class="record-list">
				<li class="record-row">
					<span class="record-year">2017</span>
					<span class="record-item">
						<span class="cite-title">&ldquo;Authorship in the Age of Digital Archiving.&rdquo;</span>
						<span class="record-note">Technology, Knowledge, and Society International Conference &middot; Toronto,&nbsp;Canada</span>
					</span>
				</li>
				<li class="record-row">
					<span class="record-year">2012</span>
					<span class="record-item">
						<span class="cite-title">&ldquo;Shadowy Margins and Sinkholes: Authorship, Online Archives, and IP&nbsp;Law.&rdquo;</span>
						<span class="record-note">Popular and American Culture Association &middot; Boston,&nbsp;MA</span>
					</span>
				</li>
				<li class="record-row">
					<span class="record-year">2011</span>
					<span class="record-item">
						<span class="cite-title">&ldquo;The Rhetoric of Road-Rage: Authorial Websites and Legal Hit-and-Runs.&rdquo;</span>
						<span class="record-note">Popular and American Culture Association &middot; San&nbsp;Antonio,&nbsp;TX</span>
					</span>
				</li>
				<li class="record-row">
					<span class="record-year">2008</span>
					<span class="record-item">
						<span class="cite-title">&ldquo;Intellectual Properties: Growth Boundaries in Digital Scholarship and Copyright Law.&rdquo;</span>
						<span class="record-note">Southwest/Texas Popular Culture Association &middot; Albuquerque,&nbsp;NM</span>
					</span>
				</li>
				<li class="record-row">
					<span class="record-year">2007</span>
					<span class="record-item">
						<span class="cite-title">&ldquo;Digital Humanizing: Administering Author/Scholar Relationships.&rdquo;</span>
						<span class="record-note">Society for Digital Humanities &middot; Saskatoon,&nbsp;Canada</span>
					</span>
				</li>
				<li class="record-row">
					<span class="record-year">2007</span>
					<span class="record-item">
						<span class="cite-title">&ldquo;Administering Author Websites: Evolving Challenges in Scholarship, Copyright, and Discussion.&rdquo;</span>
						<span class="record-note">Popular and American Culture Association &middot; Boston,&nbsp;MA</span>
					</span>
				</li>
			</ul>

			<figure class="site-shot site-shot-last">
				<Image
					src={maw2009}
					alt="The Martin Amis Web home page in 2009: a brown table layout with the site's title in a dark banner, an author portrait and a book-cover slideshow, and three rows of section links above a short introduction"
					widths={[480, 768, 1152, 1536]}
					sizes="(max-width: 53rem) 90vw, 48rem"
				/>
				<figcaption>The 2006 design, as archived in 2009</figcaption>
			</figure>
		</section>

		<section class="archive">
			<h2>Other Sites</h2>
			<p>
				Besides the Martin Amis Web, I have designed and administered three
				iterations of my personal webpage, plus three institutional sites: the WOU
				Honors&nbsp;Program (2008&ndash;&#8288;2026), Faculty Senate (2010&ndash;&#8288;2012), and
				English Department (2007&ndash;&#8288;2009) webpages.
			</p>
			<p>
				I started programming in 1982, on an Apple&nbsp;IIe, in BASIC. I once
				asked a&nbsp;mentor whether I should major in computer science in college; his
				reply&nbsp;was, &ldquo;No, stick with social science or the humanities &mdash;
				there&rsquo;s no jobs in computers.&rdquo; Choose your mentors wisely.
			</p>
		</section>
	</div>
</Layout>

<style>
	.page-head {
		padding-block: clamp(3.5rem, 9vw, 6.5rem) clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 3rem);
	}

	.page-head h1 {
		margin: 0 0 1rem;
	}

	.page-intro {
		font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 1rem + 0.6vw, 1.3rem);
		line-height: 1.5;
		color: var(--color-ink-soft);
		margin: 0;
		max-width: 42rem;
		text-wrap: balance;
	}

	.archive {
		padding-block: clamp(2.25rem, 5vw, 3.25rem);
		border-top: 1px solid var(--color-line);
	}

	.archive h2 {
		margin: 0 0 1.35rem;
	}

	/* Plain wrapping, not the site-wide `pretty`. These paragraphs are four
	   or five lines, and `pretty` re-sets short paragraphs whole, pulling
	   words down early: "Honors / Program", "a / mentor", "reply / was",
	   with lines ending 70-90px short of the measure. */
	.archive p {
		margin: 0 0 1.15rem;
		max-width: 42rem;
		text-wrap: wrap;
	}

	.archive p:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	/* screenshots span the column, right edge on the 1024 rule */
	.site-shot {
		margin: 2rem 0 2.25rem;
	}

	.site-shot-last {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	.site-shot a {
		display: block;
		line-height: 0;
	}

	.site-shot img {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
		border: 1px solid var(--color-line-strong);
		border-radius: 2px;
	}

	.site-shot figcaption {
		margin-top: 0.6rem;
		text-align: center;
		font-family: var(--font-sans);
		font-size: 0.78rem;
		letter-spacing: 0.02em;
		color: var(--color-ink-faint);
	}

	.archive .record-list {
		margin-top: 1.5rem;
	}

	/* short records take the full column, landing on the 1024 rule with the
	   screenshots; continuous prose keeps the 42rem measure */
	.record-list {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
	}

	.record-row {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 5rem 1fr;
		gap: 0 1.5rem;
		padding-block: 0.75rem;
		align-items: baseline;
	}

	.record-row + .record-row {
		border-top: 1px solid var(--color-line);
	}

	.record-year {
		font-family: var(--font-sans);
		font-size: 0.8rem;
		color: var(--color-ink-faint);
		font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
	}

	.record-item {
		font-size: 1rem;
		line-height: 1.45;
		color: var(--color-ink-soft);
		text-wrap: pretty;
	}

	.record-note {
		display: block;
		margin-top: 0.2rem;
		font-family: var(--font-sans);
		font-size: 0.78rem;
		color: var(--color-ink-faint);
	}

	.cite-title {
		color: var(--color-ink);
	}

	@media (max-width: 34rem) {
		.record-row {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
			gap: 0.25rem;
		}
	}
</style>
